 | `purge.py` | CDN purge client |
 
 Run the worker with `python worker.py`. Tests: `pytest -q`.
+
+## Constraints
+
+Four values below look arbitrary or wrong against public documentation. Each is
+the fix for an incident that reached customers. Read the note before changing
+the line.
+
+### `Pillow==10.2.0` is pinned for a reason nobody has identified
+
+A bump to 10.3.0 shipped and ran for a week before a customer reported that
+`md` thumbnails were visibly softer. Reverting to 10.2.0 fixed it. The pin
+stays.
+
+The explanation recorded at the time was that 10.3.0 changed which resampling
+filter `Image.thumbnail()` picks when no `resample=` is passed. That is not
+what happened, and the difference matters:
+
+- `Image.thumbnail()` declares `resample=Resampling.BICUBIC` as its default in
+  both 10.2.0 and 10.3.0 — there is no implicit, version-dependent choice.
+- `src/libImaging/Resample.c` and `Reduce.c` are identical across the two tags;
+  the only change inside `thumbnail()` is a type annotation.
+- Running `render.thumb()`'s exact `md` call under both versions gives
+  byte-identical output for RGB, JPEG, palette, grayscale, CMYK and RGBA
+  sources.
+
+So the softness is real but its cause is unknown, and the fix a future agent
+will reach for first — pass an explicit `resample=` and call the pin
+redundant — addresses nothing. It is a no-op against a mechanism we have not
+found.
+
+Clearing this needs the one comparison nobody has run: take real production
+uploads, render `md` under 10.2.0 and 10.3.0, and look at the results. If they
+match, the cause is outside Pillow and the pin is not the control anyone thinks
+it is. If they differ, that finally reproduces February and the difference can
+be chased.
+
+### `purge.CHUNK = 50` overrides the documented limit
+
+The CDN documents 500 URLs per purge call. Batches past a couple of hundred
+returned `429`. Their support engineer said on a call that 500 is the
+enterprise plan and ours caps at "around fifty"; `CHUNK = 50` stopped the
+`429`s.
+
+The whole basis is that one verbal statement plus the errors going away. It has
+never been reproduced deliberately and no vendor document mentions fifty — so
+reading 500 in their docs is not new information and is not a reason to raise
+`CHUNK`. Raise it only against written terms for our own plan, or a deliberate
+reproduction on it.
+
+### Nothing outside `worker.py` may delete from `jobs/`
+
+`accept.py` writes `jobs/<id>.json` at the moment an upload is accepted;
+`worker.drain()` removes it once the thumbnails are out and the purge has
+returned. Between those two points that file is the only record anywhere that
+the upload was accepted — no database row, no queue entry, no log to replay.
+
+So a file's age says nothing about whether its job is live; an old one is a job
+still in flight or one that failed and is waiting to be retried. A cleanup
+script reaping `jobs/*.json` older than an hour during a drain destroyed eleven
+uploads, which were only recovered by asking those customers to send them
+again. This constraint ends when an accepted upload is recorded somewhere
+durable outside `jobs/`, and not before.
+
+### Logging, never `print()`
+
+No `print()` anywhere in this repo — use the `logging` module.
+
+`worker.py` logs through a module logger and configures nothing itself.
+Whatever calls `drain()` must call `logging.basicConfig()`, or its `INFO` lines
+are discarded silently.
+
+## Tests
+
+`pytest -q` runs `test_guards.py`, which asserts the two constants above still
+hold and that no `print()` has appeared. The failure messages say what to
+verify. They read source text rather than importing, so they need no
+third-party packages.
